A||N||7||bilbo||Manifold studs??? Best way to remove???||||1||
Z||000000||bilbo||06-23-2001||10:08 AM||||I'm all ready to go on the manifolds I have the saturn studs and nuts.But something I didn't take into consideration is I know you use a torx socket for the saturn studs but what do I use to remove the original studs.And whats the easiest way of doing this,I know to soak with liquid wrench overnite but what tool do I use to remove studs? Please tell me it's not visegrips!!!!!Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||bilbo||06-23-2001||10:55 AM||||Shameless bump!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||06-23-2001||02:43 PM||||The original are not studs they are bolts. But to remove a stud you can use two nuts. Lock them together on the stud and make them into a bolt. Or get a stud extactor. There are a couple of different kinds. Or yes vise grips and bloody knuckles.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||06-23-2001||06:34 PM||||Use a set of chattery Teeth or dentures, and duct-tape on a cheater-bar... Don't forget the Fixodent!<P>Yeah, two nuts will work... One will tighten up against the other and stop moving, allowing you to loosen [break] the studs at your leisure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>No, seriously, that's the way to go, unless you like bloody knuckles. Of course, you could also just drill 'em out. Or not  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||bilbo||06-23-2001||07:01 PM||||Thanks Guy's! Not looking forward to doing it but...it's gotta be done.||||reg||14
Z||000005||rogergarrison||06-23-2001||07:37 PM||||if there still the stock OEM bolts (not studs) soak them with wd40 AFTER IT COOLS, overnite. Also best to start with a freshly cleaned engine to get all the oil & grime off first. I think they may be 12MM, try to loosen them easily-dont use a ton of pressure, there really small and break off pretty easy. If they break loose, work them in and back out a little at a time till they turn ez-then throw them all away. If theyre too tite, try using a little heat from a torch on them, then rapping the head with a hammer to try and knock the rusted/seized bolt loose. If it does break, have fun, youll need and ez out, angle drill, and a tap set.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mickey_Moose||06-24-2001||12:39 PM||||I have just recently completed this task myself (88 GT)and found that the bolts came out easily. Soak and let stand overnight in a good penatrating oil. If they are tight, turn them in a bit then out. The left motor side (car front) lower ones are way easier to get out from under the car. I did have to drill one out, but it was broken before I started. They are a relative soft metal bolt so they do break easy so watch...but they also drill out easy as well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I was dreading the process as well, but had them out in less than 2 hours including the time to drill out the one that was broken. Most of my time other than that was spent in reaming out the manifold and getting them welded up and ceramic coated.<P>good luck<P>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000007||bilbo||06-24-2001||07:52 PM||||Thanks again guy's for all your help!!!||||reg||1
